RETROACTIVE LOG — commit 3b8bb7b, recorded after the fact (the changelog/ convention
was introduced later, in commit 478c817).

File: vUX-spaEngine.js

=======================================================================
Section 1 — route exit hook
OLD_SECTION
(no exit notification existed; content was replaced directly, e.g.)
container.innerHTML = content;
...
defaultContentNode.innerHTML = data.content;

NEW_SECTION
var exitCallback=null, activeRoute=null;
...
function fireExitCallback(){
    // Notify the outgoing route before its content is replaced, so pages can release timers/listeners
    if (activeRoute == null) return;

    let usedExitCallback    = null;
    let outgoingRoute       = routeConfigs.routes[activeRoute.name];

    if (outgoingRoute != undefined && outgoingRoute.exitCallback != undefined){
        usedExitCallback = outgoingRoute.exitCallback;
    }else if (exitCallback != null){
        usedExitCallback = exitCallback;
    }

    if (usedExitCallback != null) usedExitCallback(activeRoute.name, activeRoute.data);
}
(fireExitCallback() + activeRoute bookkeeping added at every content-replacement
site: insertContent() both branches, the history-transition path, and boot — which
now routes through insertContent(defaultContentNode, data.content, null).)

REASON
Pages had no way to release timers, observers or components when their content was
about to be replaced. routes.<name>.exitCallback (falling back to config.exitCallback)
now fires just before replacement — on click navigation, history transitions and boot
— receiving (routeName, params) of the outgoing route.

=======================================================================
Section 2 — getDynamicData(): segment-count mismatch
OLD_SECTION
}else{
    status = false;
}

NEW_SECTION
}else{
    state.status = false;
}

REASON
The branch handling a segment-count mismatch assigned the undeclared identifier
'status' (silently landing on window.status in a browser) instead of state.status,
so any requested route whose segment count differed from a dynamic pattern was
treated as MATCHING it with empty params — e.g. /table matched /user/;id:[0-9]+ and
served the user route's cached content.
